    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a clear emphasis on speed and favorable matchups against back-end rotation arms. Our strongest play is **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13), who occupies the leadoff spot against Ritchie. Between the favorable xERA of 4.96 and the premium \"Order #1\" multiplier, he is our prime candidate for stolen base production today. **Trea Turner** (79.76) follows closely behind, capitalizing on a soft matchup against the southpaw Simpson; his dynamic platoon edge and secondary leadoff-adjacent ranking make him a high-floor anchor for our 5x5 Roto effort. **Colt Keith** (69.64) also warrants attention, as he faces the vulnerable Scherzer with the added benefit of a slow-delivery pitcher that boosts his projected stolen base efficiency by 2%.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Caleb Durbin** (55.43), who sits just outside the active roster despite a profile that historically thrives in our stolen base-heavy system. While **Caleb Durbin** benefited from the same \"Slow SP Delivery\" bonus as **Colt Keith**, the optimization model ultimately favored the higher-order bats of **Lars Nootbaar** and **Carson Benge**, whose primary lineup positioning outweighed the marginal utility of **Caleb Durbin**\u2019s bench projection. Meanwhile, **Junior Caminero** (59.06) remains in the lineup despite a slightly suppressed score caused by park factors; we are gambling on his raw output in the #3 spot over the more volatile, lower-ranked options on the bench like **Pedro Ramirez** (54.35) or **Chase Meidroth** (50.09).\n\nManagement must monitor the weather and official lineup confirmations closely, as several of our mid-tier bats\u2014specifically **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (58.43)\u2014are teetering on the edge of the 50-point mark. While **Yordan Alvarez** (68.26) provides a high ceiling, his platoon disadvantage against the lefty Lynch IV is a minor point of concern, though his elite 0.443 xwOBA keeps his floor high enough to warrant a start. We are avoiding **Jacob Gonzalez** (33.61) entirely today, as the combination of a tough matchup against Henderson and an unfavorable #8 lineup spot places him well below our roster's efficiency floor.",
